Because the line uses `"interpolate": "linear"` with a null April value, the two segments would still be joined. To avoid inventing a value across April, the line layer should break at null. Vega-Lite does this by default when `"invalid": "break-paths-show-domains"` is set on the mark or the encoding's field is null with `"defined"` handling, so the safer choice is to draw the line for the five real months with a separate series per contiguous run:

```json
{
  "data": {"values": [
    {"label": "Jan", "signups": 420, "run": "a", "order": 1},
    {"label": "Feb", "signups": 465, "run": "a", "order": 2},
    {"label": "Mar", "signups": 510, "run": "a", "order": 3},
    {"label": "May", "signups": 560, "run": "b", "order": 5},
    {"label": "Jun", "signups": 600, "run": "b", "order": 6}
  ]},
  "mark": {"type": "line", "point": true, "color": "#1f4e79"},
  "encoding": {
    "x": {"field": "label", "type": "ordinal", "sort": {"field": "order"}, "axis": {"title": "Month (2026)"}},
    "y": {"field": "signups", "type": "quantitative", "scale": {"domainMin": 0}, "axis": {"title": "Sign-ups per month (count)"}},
    "detail": {"field": "run"}
  }
}
```

With `detail` set to the run, March and May are never joined, so the gap at April stays a gap. The "No data" label sits on the x-axis at April.

